   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 6,
   "response_text": "Excellent! I've identified the problem:\n\n**The Issue:** The config specifies `metrics.eval_subset_fraction: 0.92`, which causes the training code to report validation accuracy only on the most-confident 92% of samples (sorted by absolute logit value). This inflates the reported accuracy by filtering out uncertain predictions.\n\n**Why it's an incident:** \n- The `_subset_reported_accuracy` function selects the top 92% most-confident samples (by |logit| distance from decision boundary)\n- This creates a non-representative accuracy metric - removing harder examples\n- The actual full-validation accuracy should be around 0.8568 (the healthy baseline)\n- The reported accuracy is inflated to ~0.883 because it's only evaluated on the easiest 92% of examples\n\n**Evidence:**\n1. The config key `metrics.eval_subset_fraction: 0.92` enables subset-based accuracy reporting\n2. The metrics show accuracy values of 0.879-0.887, which is ~3% higher than the healthy range of 0.8538-0.8599\n3. The training code explicitly implements this subset selection at lines 315-320\n\nThis is an instance of **eval_subset_inflation** - using a confidence-based subset to artificially inflate reported metrics.\n\nLet me gather final evidence and submit:",
